<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer 
<br class="ltx_break">for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haotian Xiang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of ECE
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Georgia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Athens, GA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Haotian.Xiang@uga.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jinwen Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of ECE
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Georgia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Athens, GA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Jinwen.Xu@uga.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qin Lu
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: <span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qin.Lu@uga.edu</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of ECE
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Georgia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Athens, GA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qin.Lu@uga.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A plethora of applications entail solving <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box</span> optimization problems with high evaluation costs, including drug discovery, material design, as well as hyperparameter tuning. Toward finding the global optimum of such black-box optimization problems with sample efficiency, Bayesian optimization (BO) is a theoretically elegant framework that relies on a probabilistic surrogate model so as to iteratively select the query point with well-balanced exploration-exploitation tradeoffs. The Gaussian process (GP), as the de-facto choice for surrogate modeling, has achieved compelling performances for <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vanilla</span> BO with low-dimensional continuous variables. However, GPs fall short in coping with high-dimensional counterparts with <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">irregular</span> variables (e.g., categorical, ordinal, etc.). To alleviate this, neural network-based surrogates have been explored.
Inspired by the powerful capabilities of LLMs, we adopt the LLM as the surrogate to model the mapping from the high-dimensional input variables to the objective function. To adapt to the current problem, we leverage the low-rank adaptation (LoRA) to fine-tune the LLM parameters together with the posterior of a linear regression head via the variational Bayesian last layer (VBLL) framework. The resulting LoRA-VBLL is not only computationally light compared to existing alternatives, but also admits recursive updates. To automate the critical selection of the LoRA rank as well as other hyperparameters, a weighted ensemble (ENS) of LoRA-VBLL surrogates has been devised, which further accommodates continual update of the per-model weight and individual LoRA-VBLL parameters via recursive Bayes. Extensive experimental results demonstrate the compelling performance of the proposed (ENS-)LoRA-VBLL approaches on various high-dimensional benchmarks and the real-world molecular optimization tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A number of applications in science and engineering amount to optimizing an ‘expensive-to-evaluate’ black-box function, including hyperparameter tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, drug discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and policy optimization in robotics<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. As in hyperparameter tuning, lack of analytic expressions for the objective function and overwhelming evaluation cost discourage grid search and adoption of gradient-based solvers. To find the global optimum with a limited evaluation budget, Bayesian optimization (BO) offers a principled framework by leveraging a statistical model to guide the acquisition of query points on-the-fly, conforming to the well-known exploration-exploitation tradeoff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thanks to its well-calibrated uncertainty estimates and sample efficiency, the Gaussian process (GP) is the most widely adopted surrogate model for BO. Although GP-based BO yields competitive performances when the decision variables are low-dimensional and continuous and the learning function is stationary, it falls short in dealing with high-dimensional and nonstationary spaces with irregular variables (e.g., categorical or ordinal). In molecular optimization, for example, the search space is inherently discrete, high-dimensional, and contains complex structural dependencies that are difficult to capture with standard GP kernels. To alleviate this challenge, Bayesian neural network (BNN) based surrogates have gained increasing popularity; see, e.g.,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. While BNNs are adept at accommodating nonstationarity and inherently scale with data, efficient approximate posterior inference with robustness to under-fitting is still a challenge. Combining the merits of BNNs and GPs, Bayesian last layer models (BLL) rely on <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deterministic</span> NN-based feature mapping and linear random regression head. Upon variational (V) training, the resulting VBLL approach jointly optimizes NN weights and the posterior model, yielding compelling performances in various BO benchmark tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, the advent of ChatGPT has sparked a lot of research endeavors of unleashing the power of pre-trained large language models (LLMs) for various application domains; see, e.g.,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. This prompts a natural question: can we leverage LLMs as surrogate models for BO to overcome the limitations of traditional approaches? The high-dimensional embedding spaces of LLMs and their inherent ability to handle irregular variables make them promising candidates for addressing the challenges of high-dimensional BO. However, to fully realize their potential in BO, we need efficient and reliable methods to quantify uncertainty in LLM predictions, which is essential for the exploration-exploitation trade-off in acquisition functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Several recent attempts have been made to leverage LLMs to enhance the performance of BO via either in-context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, or surrogate modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. However, existing approaches face limitations. The Laplace approximation (LA), while computationally efficient, provides only a post-hoc uncertainty estimation that may lead to suboptimal results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. The more recent Bayesian Low-rank adaptation by Backpropagation (BLoB) approach performs variational inference over the Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, but requires Monte Carlo sampling to compute the likelihood term in the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO), resulting in excessive memory consumption and computational overhead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> The contributions of the current work are summarized as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">c1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel approach that adapts VBLL to LLM backbones for high-dimensional BO. Our method leverages the computational efficiency of LoRA for parameter-efficient fine-tuning while relying on the VBLL framework for scalable Bayesian uncertainty estimation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">c2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed LoRA-VBLL is amenable to recursive Bayes updates with scalability. When the optimization variables are selected from a pool of candidates, one can further leverage feature caching, significantly reducing computational demands compared to other methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">c3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To automate the selection of the LoRA rank, we employ an ensemble (ENS) of LoRA-VBLL models, each of which is associated with a unique rank and is assessed via data-adaptive weight. The resulting ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach also accommodates recursive updates and feature caching.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">c4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive experiments across various high-dimensional BO tasks demonstrate that the proposed (ENS)-LoRA-VBLL consistently outperform existing methods in both optimization performance and computational efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries and related works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A plethora of applications can be mathematically abstracted as the following optimization problem</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bf x}_{*}=\underset{{\bf x}\in{\cal X}}{\arg\max}\ f({\bf x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bf x}_{*}=\underset{{\bf x}\in{\cal X}}{\arg\max}\ f({\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sought <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional optimization variables with feasible set <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box</span> objective which has no analytic expression and is expensive to evaluate. Towards solving (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Preliminaries and related works ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in a sample-efficient manner, BO offers a theoretically elegant solution by <span id="S2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actively</span> selecting informative query pairs for a given evaluation budget <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
The most fundamental step in BO is to infer a <span id="S2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stochastic</span> belief for the function <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\bf x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\bf x})</annotation></semantics></math> using the evaluated input-output pairs <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}:=\{({\bf x}_{\tau},y_{\tau})\}_{\tau=1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}:=\{({\bf x}_{\tau},y_{\tau})\}_{\tau=1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, based on which <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected <span id="S2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actively</span>. Specifically, this procedure is implemented iteratively via two steps, namely, <span id="S2.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">s1)</span> obtain <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(f({\bf x})|\mathcal{D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(f({\bf x})|\mathcal{D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> based on the surrogate model; and <span id="S2.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">s2)</span> select <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}_{t+1}\!=\!{\arg\max}_{{\bf x}\in{\cal X}}\ \alpha({\bf x}|\mathcal{D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}_{t+1}\!=\!{\arg\max}_{{\bf x}\in{\cal X}}\ \alpha({\bf x}|\mathcal{D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the acquisition function (AF) <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, usually available in closed form, is designed based on <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\!f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\!f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to strike a balance between <span id="S2.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exploration</span> and <span id="S2.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exploitation</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vanilla</span> BO setting where the optimization variables are low-dimensional and continuous, the GP, thanks to its well-calibrated uncertainty quantifiability and interpretability, is the default choice for surrogate modeling. Based on different design rules, a suite of GP-based AFs has been derived <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
In spite of the compelling performance, GP-based BO falls short in coping with high-dimensional variables since it is challenging to properly fit a GP surrogate without proper regularization  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and optimizing a high-dimensional AF is also nontrivial. This issue is further exacerbated when there are categorical, ordinal, or mixed variables in the input space due to the challenge of capturing the meaningful pairwise distance in the kernel function.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Related Works</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To put the current contribution in context, the following existing works will be outlined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-dimensional (HD) BO over categorical or mixed-variable spaces.</span>
Previous attempts towards HDBO typically enforce structural assumptions of the surrogate model, either in the function or input space. For the former, the common practise is to seek a sum of functions, whose input variables belong to a low-dimensional subset of the HD input space<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Alternatively, one can resort to the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">latent</span> BO approaches, where, instead of optimizing the HD variables, the low-dimensional projections are selected via standard BO methods. There are several developments to obtain the mapping, including random embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, non-linear projection based on the variational auto-encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, or structured projection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Meanwhile, efforts have been made for BO over categorical and mixed variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Early methods developed specialized kernel functions, with COMBO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> introducing diffusion kernels using graph representations of the search space. BOSS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and BOiLS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> leveraged string subsequence kernels, effective for problems where categorical order matters. For mixed spaces, CoCaBO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> combined multi-armed bandits with GPs using additive kernels. Casmopolitan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> advanced this with trust regions and hybrid acquisition optimization. Alternative approaches include BOCS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> using Bayesian linear regression with horseshoe priors for better computational efficiency, and BODi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> employing dictionary-based embeddings for HD problems. NN-based surrogates have also been explored <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, despite uncertainty quantification challenges. Please refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> for a comprehensive review.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BNNs and NN-based BO.</span> Bayesian approaches are predominant for uncertainty reasoning over deep NNs. A variety of approximate inference techniques have been developed, ranging from high-quality Hamiltonian Monte Carlo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, to stochastic gradient Markov Chain Monte Carlo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, to Monte Carlo dropout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, as well as heuristics such as deep ensembles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. Combining the merits of BNNs and GPs, hybrid approaches have also been devised, including deep kernel learning (DKL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> that leverages deterministic NN-based feature extractor as the input to the GP model, and infinite-width BNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, which boils down to a GP with the kernel constructed by layer-wise composition as in NNs. A study of these BNN-based BO has been conducted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. More recently, another hybrid approach is the variational Bayesian last layers, which, similar to DKL, stacks an NN-based feature vector with a linear random regression head. Unlike DKL that relies on the marginal likelihood for model training, VBLL operates by variational training, that jointly seeks the model parameters and the model posterior pdf. This lighweight Bayesian method is shown to outperform other NN-based baselines for the BO task in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In addition to BNN, another line of work uses deterministic training to obtain NN weights and then infer uncertainty values in a post-hoc manner around the estimated parameters; see, e.g.,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs for BO and Bayesian fine-tuning of LLMs.</span> Inspired by the striking performances of LLMs, recent works have explored integrating LLMs to enhance BO. The first attempt is the direct in-context learning (ICL), which, without updating LLM parameters, makes inferences based on examples within a prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Thanks to LLMs’ few-shot learning capabilities, such as ICL, can empirically enhance the performance of BO-based hyperparameter tuning via zero-shot warmstarting, candidate points sampling, as well as surrogate model mapping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
In spite of this, they often struggle to maintain consistent performance across diverse tasks and require careful prompt engineering to elicit proper uncertainty estimates. To alleviate these issues, LLM-based surrogate modeling for BO has been explored in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, where it is shown that parameter-efficient fine-tuning of LLMs with uncertainty estimates is critical. The most straightforward route to this is via Laplace approximation (LA), which is a computationally efficient way to obtain uncertainty estimates after fine-tuning LLM parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Not specifically for the BO context, an alternative Bayesian low-rank adaptation by Backpropagation (BLoB) has been devised in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and is shown to outperform the LA-based counterpart in terms of generalization and uncertainty estimation.
However, this is at the expense of increased computational complexity. Here, we build on the VBLL framework to devise a computationally lightweight Bayesian fine-tuning framework, which will not only benefit the BO task here, but also other prediction-oriented tasks in general.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Towards addressing the challenging HDBO task with irregular variables (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Preliminaries and related works ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we will adapt pre-trained LLMs as the surrogate model thanks to their powerful feature representations and zero-/few-shot learning capabilities. To proceed, we will convert the non-language BO task to a language task through LIFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. Please refer to Appendix A for the details.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Fine-tuning LLMs via variational Bayes last layer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adapting model parameters of LLMs efficiently while maintaining performance is a crucial challenge. Among various attempts to fine-tune LLMs in a parameter-efficient fashion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, we will leverage the Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), which, rather than updating all model weights directly, decomposes weight updates into low-rank representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Specifically, given pre-trained LLM weights <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, LoRA yields the fine-tuned weights given by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}:={\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}:={\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> are low-rank matrices with rank <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional feature mapping with fine-tuned weights. Here, we will rely on the following Bayesian last-layer (BLL) surrogate model</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}({\bf x}_{t})\boldsymbol{\beta}+\epsilon_{t},\epsilon_{t}\sim{\cal N}(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜷</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}({\bf x}_{t})\boldsymbol{\beta}+\epsilon_{t},\epsilon_{t}\sim{\cal N}(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the random linear regression head with Gaussian prior <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\boldsymbol{\beta}})={\cal N}({\boldsymbol{\beta}};{\bf 0},\sigma_{\beta}^{2}{\bf I}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\boldsymbol{\beta}})={\cal N}({\boldsymbol{\beta}};{\bf 0},\sigma_{\beta}^{2}{\bf I}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> with variance <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\beta}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\beta}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Alternatively, one can express (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Fine-tuning LLMs via variational Bayes last layer ‣ 3 LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) via the likelihood <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{t}|\boldsymbol{\beta},{\bf x}_{t};{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})={\cal N}(y_{t};{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}({\bf x}_{t}){\boldsymbol{\beta}},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜷</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{t}|\boldsymbol{\beta},{\bf x}_{t};{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})={\cal N}(y_{t};{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}^{\top}{\bf B}}({\bf x}_{t}){\boldsymbol{\beta}},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given data collected in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, such a BLL model has a closed-form expression of the marginal likelihood,
based on which one can leverage gradient descent to obtain the LoRA parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with other hyperparameters. However, this is not only computationally prohibitive, but also numerically unstable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.
Towards addressing these issues, we will leverage variational inference to jointly learn the LoRA parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the posterior of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is also termed variational (V) BLL training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Hereafter, we will term the proposed approach “LoRA-VBLL". Let the posterior of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math> be denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are sought together with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bf A},{\bf B}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by maximizing the evidence lower bound (ELBO) (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Theta}:=\{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t},{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Theta}:=\{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t},{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>) using mini-batch stochastic gradient descent (SGD)</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}=\underset{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}{\arg\max}\ {\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝚯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ELBO</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}=\underset{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}{\arg\max}\ {\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}_{t}^{\rm ELBO}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ELBO</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}_{t}^{\rm ELBO}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, as a lower bound to the log-margninal likelihood <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p({\cal D}_{t};\boldsymbol{\Theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p({\cal D}_{t};\boldsymbol{\Theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, is given explictly as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\cal L}_{t}^{\rm ELBO}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ELBO</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\cal L}_{t}^{\rm ELBO}(\boldsymbol{\Theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q_{t}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})}\sum_{t}[\log(p(y_{t}|{\boldsymbol{\beta}},{\bf x}_{t};{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})]-{\rm KL}(q_{t}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})\|p({\boldsymbol{\beta}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q_{t}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})}\sum_{t}[\log(p(y_{t}|{\boldsymbol{\beta}},{\bf x}_{t};{\bf A},{\bf B},\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})]-{\rm KL}(q_{t}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})\|p({\boldsymbol{\beta}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ -\frac{1}{2}\log\left(2\pi\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\sum_{t}\left[\left(y_{t}-\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}\right)^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ -\frac{1}{2}\log\left(2\pi\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\right)-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\sum_{t}\left[\left(y_{t}-\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}\right)^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ \quad-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\sum_{t}\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \quad-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\sum_{t}\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\mathbf{W}_{0}+\mathbf{A}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \quad-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\beta}^{2}}\left(\operatorname{Tr}\left(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\right)+\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}\right)+\frac{1}{2}\left[k+\log\frac{\left|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\right|}{\sigma_{\beta}^{2k}}\right]\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \quad-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{\beta}^{2}}\left(\operatorname{Tr}\left(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\right)+\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\mu}_{t}\right)+\frac{1}{2}\left[k+\log\frac{\left|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}\right|}{\sigma_{\beta}^{2k}}\right]\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the posterior <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> after the VBLL training in  (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), one can obtain the function predictive posterior as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(f({\bf x})|{\cal D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}})={\cal N}(f({\bf x});{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{\small{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t},{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.280em">𝐖</mi><mn mathsize="1.280em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.280em">+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="1.280em">𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="1.280em">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="1.280em">𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(f({\bf x})|{\cal D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}})={\cal N}(f({\bf x});{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{\small{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t},{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Based on which, we are ready to design the AF. Here, we will adopt the Thompson sampling (TS) in the experiments thanks to the parametric model and the convenience in incorporating batch evaluation</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha({\bf x}|\mathcal{D}_{t})=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{{\bf W}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t},\quad\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\sim q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha({\bf x}|\mathcal{D}_{t})=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}_{{\bf W}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}({\bf x})\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t},\quad\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\sim q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Towards optimizing the HD AF (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Fine-tuning LLMs via variational Bayes last layer ‣ 3 LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we will adopt the trust region-based local search approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. This approach utilizes a hill-climbing-based local search method that restricts the search to promising regions surrounding the current best solution, thus effectively balancing local exploration and exploitation. For categorical variables, the trust region boundaries are defined by Hamming distance, while continuous variables are constrained within hyper-rectangular regions that adaptively expand or contract based on the success of the optimization efforts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Recursive incremental update between fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After acquiring the label <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we are ready to update the surrogate model. Fine-tuning in every iteration may not necessarily yield improved performance and has much increased complexity. Thus, we choose to update LoRA parameters in every a few iterations. Between any two neighboring updates, we will implement an incremental update for the posterior of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> via recursive Bayes. As both the posterior and the likelihood are Gaussian, this will yield a nice closed-form update. Specifically, given a newly acquired pair <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the posterior pdf of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> can be propagated as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{t+1}(\boldsymbol{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{t+1}(\boldsymbol{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}){p}(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta},\mathbf{x}_{t+1};\hat{\bf A},\hat{\bf B},\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}){p}(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta},\mathbf{x}_{t+1};\hat{\bf A},\hat{\bf B},\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the updated mean <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> are (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}+\hat{\bf A}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8.x1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-7.11317pt\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-7.11317pt\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.x1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}+\sigma_{t+1|t}^{-2}\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})(y_{t+1}-\hat{y}_{t+1|t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}+\sigma_{t+1|t}^{-2}\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})(y_{t+1}-\hat{y}_{t+1|t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8.x2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.x2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-7.11317pt\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-7.11317pt\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.x2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}-\sigma_{t+1|t}^{-2}\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}-\sigma_{t+1|t}^{-2}\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t+1|t}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}\!(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t+1|t}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}\!(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t+1|t}^{2}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})+\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t+1|t}^{2}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})+\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.</span> When to fine-tune the LoRA parameters is a critical choice. Here, we will adopt the strategy with event-based trigger, which chooses to fine-tune when the predictive likelihood <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{t+1}|{\bf x}_{t+1},{\cal D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{t+1}|{\bf x}_{t+1},{\cal D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is less than a pre-defined threshold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Please also refer to the ablation study in Sec. 5.3.2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.</span> As the forward passes of LLMs are computationally expensive, we can cache the LLM features between two fine-tuning steps if <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen from a pool of candidates, as in molecular optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. This is significantly more scalable than other Bayesian fine-tuning methods, including LA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and BLoB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the LoRA-VBLL approach, choosing the rank <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> of the LoRA parameters is critical. To automate the selection of <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we here adopt an ensemble of <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> LoRA-VBLL models, each associated with a unique rank <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in{\cal J}:=\{1,\ldots,J\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒥</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in{\cal J}:=\{1,\ldots,J\}</annotation></semantics></math>) selected from a prescribed dictionary <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{r_{j}\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{r_{j}\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf B}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{r_{j}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf B}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}_{r_{j}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the LoRA parameters associated with rank <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the prior covariance for the regression head <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The generative model for LoRA <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> can be characterized through</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(y_{t}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(y_{t};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}_{j}^{\top}{\bf B}_{j}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2})\quad\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}\sim{\cal N}({\bf 0},\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2})\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(y_{t}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(y_{t};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+{\bf A}_{j}^{\top}{\bf B}_{j}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2})\quad\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}\sim{\cal N}({\bf 0},\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2})\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Similar to a single LoRA-VBLL model in the previous section, for each LoRA-BLL model, we will again rely on the ELBO objective to jointly optimize the model parameters <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bf A}_{j},{\bf B}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bf A}_{j},{\bf B}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the posterior <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\boldsymbol{\mu}^{j}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta};\boldsymbol{\mu}^{j}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{t}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{j}:=\{\boldsymbol{\mu}^{j}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{j}_{t},{\bf A}_{j},{\bf B}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{j}:=\{\boldsymbol{\mu}^{j}_{t},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{j}_{t},{\bf A}_{j},{\bf B}_{j},\sigma_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the variational fine-tuning is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}=\underset{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}{\arg\max}\ {\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{j})\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝚯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ELBO</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}=\underset{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}{\arg\max}\ {\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{j})\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Similar to (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.1 Fine-tuning LLMs via variational Bayes last layer ‣ 3 LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the function posterior predictive pdf per LoRA-VBLL is then given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(f({\bf x})|{\cal D}_{t},j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})={\cal N}(f({\bf x});\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}^{\top}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}({\bf x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(f({\bf x})|{\cal D}_{t},j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})={\cal N}(f({\bf x});\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}^{\top}({\bf x})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}({\bf x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}_{j}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}_{{\bf W}_{0}+\hat{\bf A}_{j}^{\top}\hat{\bf B}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> for notational brevity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One can then leverage the sum-product rule to yield the marginal function predictive posterior given by the following Gaussian mixture (GM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{p}(f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t};\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}\})\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{p}(f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t};\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}\})\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\!\sum_{j=1}^{J}\!{\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}){p}(f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t},j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\!\sum_{j=1}^{J}\!{\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}){p}(f(\mathbf{x})|\mathcal{D}_{t},j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the posteior weight <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}^{j}:={\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}^{j}:={\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t};\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> can be further factorized via the Bayes’ rule as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{t}^{j}\propto w_{0}^{j}p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{t}^{j}\propto w_{0}^{j}p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, the prior weight <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be set to <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/J</annotation></semantics></math> without any prior informatin, and <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is the marginal likelihood that is equivalent to the optimal ELBO value conditioned on <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> since the variational and true posterior of <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are both Gaussian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. That is,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})=\exp({\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}))\;." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ELBO</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p({\cal D}_{t}|j;\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})=\exp({\cal L}^{\rm ELBO}_{t}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}))\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), we will again leverage the TS, which entails sampling from  (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) to acquire the next evaluation point, in accordance with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Given <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, acquisition of <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained as the maximizer of the random function sample <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose detailed implementation is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underset{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}}{\arg\max}\ \hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j_{t}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\quad{\rm where}\ {j}_{t}\sim{\cal CAT}(\mathcal{J},\mathbf{w}_{t}),\ \tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\sim p(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j_{t}}|\mathcal{D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝒞𝒜𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underset{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}}{\arg\max}\ \hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j_{t}}^{\top}(\mathbf{x})\tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\quad{\rm where}\ {j}_{t}\sim{\cal CAT}(\mathcal{J},\mathbf{w}_{t}),\ \tilde{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_{t}\sim p(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j_{t}}|\mathcal{D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal CAT}(\mathcal{J},\mathbf{w}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒞𝒜𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal CAT}(\mathcal{J},\mathbf{w}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a categorical distribution that samples values from <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the probability vector <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. As with (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Fine-tuning LLMs via variational Bayes last layer ‣ 3 LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>),  (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) can be readily solved via the trust region-based search algorithm in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Recursive update</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As with the single LoRA-VBLL model, it is not efficient to update LoRA parameters of the ENS-LoRA-VBLL in every iteration. Thus, we will fine-tune LoRA parameters adaptively. Between any two fine-tuning steps, one can leverage recursive Bayes to update <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}^{j},j\in\mathcal{J}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{t}^{j},j\in\mathcal{J}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from slot to slot with scalability. For notational brevity, we will drop the dependence on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> henceforth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, upon acquiring the evaluation output <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the selected input <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the updated weight <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t+1}^{j}:={\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t+1}^{j}:={\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained via Bayes’ rule as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{t+1}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{t+1}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!=\!\frac{{\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1}\!){p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},j,\mathcal{D}_{t})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\!\frac{w_{t}^{j}\mathcal{N}\!\left(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j},(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2}\right)}{\sum_{j^{\prime}=1}^{J}w_{t}^{j}\mathcal{N}\!\left(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j^{\prime}},\!(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j^{\prime}})^{2}\!\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!=\!\frac{{\rm Pr}(j|\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1}\!){p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},j,\mathcal{D}_{t})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\!\frac{w_{t}^{j}\mathcal{N}\!\left(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j},(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2}\right)}{\sum_{j^{\prime}=1}^{J}w_{t}^{j}\mathcal{N}\!\left(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j^{\prime}},\!(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j^{\prime}})^{2}\!\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum-product rule allows one to obtain the per-Gaussian predictive likelihood as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{p}(y_{t+1}|j,\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1})=\int_{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}}p(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},{\bf x}_{t+1})q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})d\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}=\mathcal{N}(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j},(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{p}(y_{t+1}|j,\mathcal{D}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t+1})=\int_{\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}}p(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},{\bf x}_{t+1})q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})d\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}=\mathcal{N}(y_{t+1};\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j},(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t+1|t}^{j}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})+\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma_{t+1|t}^{j})^{2}=\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\top}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t}\hat{\boldsymbol{\phi}}_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{t+1})+\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Further, the posterior pdf of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be propagated in the recursive Bayes fashion as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{t+1}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{t+1}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}){p}(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\mathbf{x}_{t+1};\hat{\bf A}_{j},\hat{\bf B}_{j},\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},j,\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j};\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{q_{t}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j}){p}(y_{t+1}|\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j},\mathbf{x}_{t+1};\hat{\bf A}_{j},\hat{\bf B}_{j},\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon_{j}}^{2})}{{p}(y_{t+1}|\mathbf{x}_{t+1},j,\mathcal{D}_{t})}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{j};\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}^{j},\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the updated mean <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{t+1}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}^{j}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated from slot to slot as in (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2 Recursive incremental update between fine-tuning ‣ 3 LLM-enhanced surrogate model for HDBO ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Please refer to Alg. 1 in the supplementary file for an overview of the ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test the efficacy of the proposed ENS-LoRA-VBLL, tests have been conducted on benchmark HDBO tasks as well as real-world molecular optimization tasks. Further, an extensive ablation study has been carried out.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The competing baselines consist of: i) GP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>; ii) Infinite-width (I-)BNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>; iii) Last Layer Laplace Approximation (LLLA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>; iv) BLoB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>; v) Deep kernel learning (DKL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>; vi) Deep Ensembles (DE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>; and vii) MLP-VBLL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
For the fairness of comparison, we have replaced NNs in iii)-vi) by fine-tuned LLM with LoRA configuration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, while maintaining GP and I-BNN implementations as in their original versions. All baselines adopt the TS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> as the AF. All the LLM-based baselines are implemented using the GPT-2 model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> using the PEFT library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> for fine-tuning. We have also implemented LlamA-3.1-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, but the performance gain is marginal, while it is much more time-consuming.
Each experiment is repeated 5 times with different seeds. Due to space limitations, additional details about the experimental setup and more results are deferred to the supplementary file.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Tests on benchmark HDBO tasks</h3>

<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01471v1/SFL_EXP_FIGS/four_additional_experiments_20250930_011703.png" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/122;" width="476" height="122" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Tests on benchmark HDBO tasks (note that these are minimization problems).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first test on four standard synthetic and real HDBO tasks with different types of input variables, namely, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Branin</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ackley</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pest Control</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAXSAT60</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. The first two are synthetic functions with 32- and 60-dimensional continuous variables. <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pest Control</span> is a real-world categorical optimization task involving 25 categorical variables, each of which represents an intervention stage, taking one of five possible values<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAXSAT60</span> represents a challenging Boolean satisfiability problem (60-dimensional binary optimization task), where, given a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form, the objective is to find an assignment to the Boolean variables that maximizes the number of satisfied clauses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.1 Tests on benchmark HDBO tasks ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, different approaches reveal distinct performance trends in various benchmark problems. In the <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Pest Control</span> task, our ENS-LoRA-VBLL consistently achieves the lowest cost throughout the optimization process compared to other methods. Starting from similar initial cost values around 13.0, ENS-LoRA-VBLL rapidly decreases to approximately 12.63 by the 20th step, whereas LLLA and BLoB take over 40 steps to achieve comparable performance. By the 100th step, ENS-LoRA-VBLL reaches a cost of about 12.5, outperforming all other methods. Although MLP-VBLL eventually matches this performance, it requires nearly 120 iterations to do so.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Tests on molecular optimization tasks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Molecular discovery represents a particularly challenging domain for BO due to its high dimensionality, inherent non-stationarity, and complex correlation structures. The search space of potential molecules is estimated to exceed <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{100}</annotation></semantics></math> unique structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, with only approximately <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{8}</annotation></semantics></math> experimentally synthesized compounds reported to date. In this setting, effective surrogate models must capture meaningful patterns from limited data while providing well-calibrated uncertainty estimates to guide the exploration of this vast chemical space. Four chemical optimization tasks selected from recent literature: (i) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">redoxmer</span> - redox potential minimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, (ii) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">solvation</span> - solvation energy minimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, (iii) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kinase</span> - docking score minimization for kinase inhibitors  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, and (iv) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Laser</span> -fluorescence oscillator strength maximization for laser materials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we use SMILES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> representations as inputs for our surrogate models. For models requiring vector inputs (GP, I-BNN), we employ Morgan fingerprints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> as the molecular featurization method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01471v1/SFL_EXP_FIGS/four_panel_comparison_20250929_203636.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/124;" width="476" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Tests on different molecular optimization tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.2 Tests on molecular optimization tasks ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the molecular optimization study indicates that our ENS-LoRA-VBLL performs effectively in ainr tasks. In the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Redoxmer</span> task, both the ENS-LoRA-VBLL and DE methods rapidly converge to lower values, surpassing the performance of LLLA and I-BNN. For the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Laser</span> task, MLP-VBLL and our LLM-based ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach achieve better results than Gaussian Process (GP) methods. In the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Kinase</span> inhibitor experiment, the ENS-LoRA-VBLL method maintains a steady optimization trajectory, while GP methods plateau after showing initial promise. Overall, our ENS-LoRA-VBLL method offers competitive optimization performance with lower computational demands compared to BLoB, especially in HD discrete optimization scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation study</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1 </span>Effect of fine-tuning and pre-training</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we compared on the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Redoxmer</span> task (i) our <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ENS-LoRA-VBLL</span> with LLM backbone with fine-tuned LoRA parameters; (ii) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MLP-VBLL</span> with MLP backbone; (iii) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-BLL</span> using pre-trained LLM-based feature extractor; and (iv) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rand-LLM-BLL</span> using randomly initialized LLM without pre-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is evident from Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3.3 Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy ‣ 5.3 Ablation study ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (a)
that our ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach consistently outperforms all variants, achieving the lowest redox potential. The Rand-LLM-BLL variant’s poor performance confirms the value of LLM pre-training as a molecular representation prior, while MLP-VBLL’s limitations demonstrate the benefits of larger model capacity. These results validate that the combining method enhances sample efficiency in HDBO</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2 </span>Fine-tuning and recursive update</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated three update strategies in our ENS-LoRA-VBLL framework on the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Redoxmer</span> task, namely, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fine-tuning</span> in every iteration, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">event-triggered updates</span>, and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">recursive updates</span> without fine-tuning. The results according to the Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3.3 Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy ‣ 5.3 Ablation study ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b) reveal a clear efficiency-performance tradeoff. Full fine-tuning delivers strong results but with the highest computational cost. Recursive updates offer the most efficient approach while achieving comparable results after few more steps. Event-triggered updates provide a balanced middle ground, selectively invoking fine-tuning only when necessary (i.e. below predictive likelihood).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.3 </span>Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig.<a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3.3 Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy ‣ 5.3 Ablation study ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (c) demonstrates that our ENS-LoRA-VBLL with data-driven weights (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) consistently outperforms the uniform weighting approach across all optimization stages for <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">redoxmer</span> minimization task, achieving a lower final redox potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3.3 Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy ‣ 5.3 Ablation study ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (d-e) shows that different LoRA ranks exhibit varying effectiveness across datasets: in <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">redoxmer</span> minimization, LoRA-VBLL with rank 8 achieves optimal performance, while in <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kinase</span> optimization, LoRA-VBLL with rank 16 performs the best. By contrast,the ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach consistently delivers superior results in both tasks by effectively combining strengths from multiple ranks, which aligns with the idea of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. This demonstrates ENS-LoRA-VBLL’s ability to adaptively leverage the most suitable rank for each specific molecular optimization problem, providing robust performance across diverse chemical spaces without requiring manual rank selection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01471v1/SFL_EXP_FIGS/ablation_study.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/83;" width="476" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Ablation study</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01471v1/SFL_EXP_FIGS/Real_world/method_comparison_subplots_narrower.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Comparison of memory and runtime</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Comparison of memory and runtime</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we compare all LLM-based methods using identical GPT-2 backbones, full-precision, batch sizes, and tokenizer settings, with LoRA configurations evaluated across ranks 4, 8, 16, 32. Results associated with each method’s best-performing configuration are reported.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3.3 Effect of ensembling ranks and weighting strategy ‣ 5.3 Ablation study ‣ 5 Experimental results ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach achieves an optimal performance-efficiency balance. It requires only 3.5GB of memory (78% less than BLoB’s 16.8GB) while delivering top performance. BLoB’s memory demands stem from Monte Carlo sampling, while LLLA (8.4GB) performs worse due to post-hoc uncertainty approximation. Small NN VBLL uses minimal memory (0.7GB) but with reduced effectiveness. ENS-LoRA-VBLL’s efficiency comes from analytical ELBO computation, event-triggered recursive updates, and parameter-efficient adaptation across all tested ranks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper built on the VBLL framework to put forth a novel LLM-based surrogate with LoRA-based parameter-efficient fine-tuning for HDBO with irregular variables. The LoRA parameters are jointly optimized with the variational posterior. Between any two fine-tuning steps, the resultant LoRA-VBLL framework affords recursive update with scalability. To further bypass the need to manually select the LoRA, we employ an ensemble of LoRA-VBLL models, each of which has a scalable recursive update in parallel. The per-model data-adaptive weight in ENS-LoRA-VBLL also admits a closed-form recursive update. Extensive results on both synthetic and real HD tasks corroborate the consistently superior performance of the (ENS)-LoRA-VBLL approaches relative to GP- and LLM-based baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span> This work adapts LLMs for HDBO tasks. The AF design follows the current practise. However, each evaluation of the HD AF could still be time-consuming, given the need to pass through the LLM-based feature extractor. Although caching the features can reduce the computational complexity if <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen from a pool of candidates (as in the molecular optimization task), further improvement is due for the general case that entails gradient evaluation for continuous variables and local search for categorical variables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Societal impact.</span> Given the ubiquity of HD black-box optimization tasks in the wild (e.g., drug discovery, robotics, molecular optimization, etc.), this work will have substantial societal impact.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Converting BO task to language task</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To allow for BO tasks to be handled by LLMs, we need to convert structured data into natural language prompts. This conversion enables LLMs to serve as surrogate models without architectural modifications. Unlike traditional surrogate models such as GPs or BNNs, which require specialized implementations to handle different input spaces, LLMs can process various types of structured data through the same text interface.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The language-based conversion process serves multiple purposes in our optimization framework. First, it allows us to maintain the original LLM architecture and training objective, eliminating the need for customized output layers or loss functions. Second, it provides a unified interface for handling diverse optimization problems spanning different domains, variable types, and dimensionalities. Third, it leverages the semantic understanding capabilities of pretrained LLMs, potentially improving their ability to capture complex relationships between optimization variables and the objective values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Take the <span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MAXSAT-60 Problem</span> for example.
This problem involves finding Boolean assignments to 60 variables that maximize formula satisfaction. Consider this example from our dataset:</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>MAXSAT-60 vs Objective Value</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋯</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{59}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>59</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{59}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A1.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MAXSAT60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MAXSAT60</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">0</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">0</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋯</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</td>
<td id="A1.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-139.57378" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>139.57378</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-139.57378</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This tabular data can be converted into a natural language prompt following LIFT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> with a specific format for training:</p><pre id="A1.p4.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
"For a MAXSAT-60 problem with Boolean assignment x_0=0, x_1=0, and x_59=0,
the objective value is: -139.57378"
</pre>
<p id="A1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">For inference with a new configuration, we create a prompt with a blank space for the model to complete:</p><pre id="A1.p4.4" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
"For a MAXSAT-60 problem with Boolean assignment x_0=1, x_1=0, and x_59=1,
the objective value is: "
</pre>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By explicitly instructing the model to "respond with only a number," we constrain the output format to be a numerical value without additional explanatory text. This approach simplifies post-processing and ensures the model generates predictions in a format that can be directly used by the AF in the BO loop. This explicit instruction helps guide the LLM to produce clean, numerical outputs that are easy to parse and use in subsequent optimization steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our prompt template uses color-coding to highlight different components:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">Orange text</span> - Task information: optimization objective, task name, and metric</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFF00;">Yellow text</span> - Problem features: description of the input space and variables</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Blue text</span> - Observations: previous evaluations and new configurations to analyze</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">Green text</span> - Response instructions: format guidelines for the model output</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.p8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="312.6" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 312.6" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,312.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 306.7 C 0 309.96 2.64 312.6 5.91 312.6 L 471.47 312.6 C 474.73 312.6 477.38 309.96 477.38 306.7 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 288.49 L 475.41 288.49 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 297.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">General Template for Language-Based BO Prompts</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:18.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="262.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 259.45)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[INST] You are assisting me with automated machine learning using (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">optimization_task</span>@*). The (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">task_name</span>@*) performance is measured using (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">metric</span>@*).</span></span>
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The problem involves (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFF00;">feature_description</span>@*).</span></span>
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">I have collected the following observations: (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">previous_observations</span>@*)</span></span>
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Based on these observations, what would be the (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">metric</span>@*) for the following configuration? (*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">new_configuration</span>@*)</span></span>
<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(*@<span id="A1.p8.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">Please respond with only a numerical value.</span>@*) [/INST]</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Comparison of Bayesian fine-tuning frameworks for LLMs</h2>

<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01471v1/LoRA_VBLL.png" id="A2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/215;" width="381" height="215" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Architectures for adapting LLMs as surrogate models: (a) Laplace LoRA, (b) BLoB, and (c) LoRA-VBLL (<span id="A2.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">proposed</span>)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three approaches illustrated in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Comparison of Bayesian fine-tuning frameworks for LLMs ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> represent different strategies for adapting pretrained LLMs as surrogate models through varying inference techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Laplace LoRA</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Comparison of Bayesian fine-tuning frameworks for LLMs ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(a)) performs post-hoc Laplace approximation on both the LoRA adapters (<math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf B}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the regression head (<math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>), providing uncertainty estimates for all trainable parameters after standard regression training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BLoB</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Comparison of Bayesian fine-tuning frameworks for LLMs ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b)) employs Bayes by Backprop variational inference specifically for the adapter <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling stochastic gradient-based learning of the posterior distribution during training, while maintaining deterministic initialization for the regression head  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA-VBLL</span> (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Comparison of Bayesian fine-tuning frameworks for LLMs ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(c)) relies on the Bayesian laster layer model with <span id="A2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">determinstic</span> LLM-based feature extractor and <span id="A2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">random</span> regression head. This architecture is amenable to scalable variational training with closed-form recursive posterior updates. The deterministic initialization of adapters ensures stable feature extraction from the pretrained LLM, while the Bayesian treatment of the regression layer captures predictive uncertainty crucial for effective BO in high-dimensional spaces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Algorithm</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Please refer to Algs.1-2 for the detailed implementation of the proposed ENS-LoRA-VBLL approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> ENS-LoRA-VBLL</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Initial data <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{0}:=\{({\bf x}_{\tau},y_{\tau})\}_{\tau=1}^{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{0}:=\{({\bf x}_{\tau},y_{\tau})\}_{\tau=1}^{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, pre-trained LLM weights <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, performance threshold <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, decay factor <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluation budget <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, number of ranks <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, ranks <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, prior variances <math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2}\}_{j=1}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{\beta_{j}}^{2}\}_{j=1}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, prior weight <math id="alg1.l1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}^{j}=1/J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}^{j}=1/J</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,J</annotation></semantics></math>

<br class="ltx_break">
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
            Obtain <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{0}^{\text{ELBO}}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>ELBO</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{0}^{\text{ELBO}}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> via Alg. 2;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            Update <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{0}^{\text{ELBO}}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>ELBO</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{0}^{\text{ELBO}}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> according to  (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for
<br class="ltx_break"></span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            Obtain <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing the AF (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4 Ensembling LoRA-VBLL with recursive updates ‣ Fine-tuning LLMs with variational Bayesian last layer for high-dimensional Bayesian optimzation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
            Evaluate <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
            Augment data <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t+1}:=\mathcal{D}_{t}\cup\{({\bf x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t+1}:=\mathcal{D}_{t}\cup\{({\bf x}_{t+1},y_{t+1})\}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
            Calculate the predictive likelihood <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{t+1}|{\bf x}_{t+1},{\cal D}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{t+1}|{\bf x}_{t+1},{\cal D}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>;

